      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve problem (2105), we use the following reasoning:\n\n**Step 1:** Determine the total number of ways all nine people can be seated around a table.\n\nSince the table is round, we fix one person's seat to eliminate over-counting due to rotational symmetry. Therefore, the remaining 8 people can be seated in 8! (8 factorial) ways.\n\n\\[\n8! = \\boxed{40320}\n\\]\n\n**Step 2:** Calculate the number of favorable arrangements where all 4 math majors sit in consecutive seats.\n\nWe treat the 4 math majors as a single block (unit). This block along with the other 5 individuals can be arranged around the table as follows:\n- Fix one person in the remaining seat to eliminate over-counting.\n- Arrange the 6 \"units\" (math majors block + 4 physics majors + 1 biology major) in 4*5! (4 factorial for the block and 5 factorial for the remaining 5 individuals) ways, since the table is circular.\n- Finally, consider the arrangement within the block of 4 math majors, which can be done in 4! ways.\n\n\\[\n\\text{Elements in a circular arrangement subtracted from total arrangements} \\times \\text{Number of ways to arrange within the block of 4 math majors} = 6 \\times 4 \\times 5! \\times 4!\n\\]\n\n**Step 3:** Compute the probability.\n\n\\[\nP(\\text{all 4 math majors sit in consecutive seats}) = \\frac{6 \\times 4 \\times 5! \\times 4!}{8!} = \\frac{6}{11}\n\\]\n\n\\boxed{\\frac{6}{11}}",
