      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats around a round table, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Total Number of Arrangements:**\n   Since it's a round table, we fix one person to avoid counting rotations as different. This means we can arrange the remaining 8 people in \\(8!\\) ways.\n\n2. **Number of Favorable Arrangements:**\n   - Treat the 4 math majors as a single \"block\". This \"block\" can be cycled in 4! ways.\n   - Once we have placed this block, we need to arrange the 5 remaining majors (3 physics majors and 2 biology majors) in the 5 remaining seats, which can be done in \\(5!\\) ways.\n   - The total number of ways to arrange the seats considering the \"math majors block\" is therefore \\(4! \\times 5! \\times 4 = 4! \\times 5! \\times 4\\).\n\nHence, the probability is given by:\n  \\[\n  P = \\frac{4! \\times 5! \\times 4}{8!}\n  \\]\n\nCalculating the factorials:\n  \\[\n  4! = 4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 24\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  5! = 5 \\times 4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 120\n  \\]\n\nPutting it all together:\n  \\[\n  P = \\frac{24 \\times 120 \\times 4}{8!} = \\frac{24 \\times 120 \\times 4}{24 \\times 720} = \\frac{120}{24 \\times 60} = \\frac{1}{10}\n  \\]\n\nThus, the probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{10}}\n\\]",
