      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Let's analyze the problem step-by-step:\n\n1. **Total Arrangements:**\n   We start by calculating the total number of ways to arrange nine people in a circle. For \\( n \\) people in a circle, the number of arrangements is \\((n-1)!\\). So, for nine people:\n   \\[\n   (9-1)! = 8! = 40320\n   \\]\n\n2. **Arranging Four Math Majors in Consecutive Seats:**\n   We can treat the four math majors as a single \"super-seat\" or \"block\". This effectively reduces the problem to arranging 6 units (the \"super-seat\" and the remaining 5 individuals). The number of ways to arrange 6 units in a circle is:\n   \\[\n   (6-1)! = 5! = 120\n   \\]\n   Within the \"super-seat\", the four math majors can be arranged among themselves in:\n   \\[\n   4! = 24 \\text{ ways}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculating the Total Number of Favorable Arrangements:**\n   The total number of favorable arrangements is the product of the number of ways to arrange the 6 units in a circle and the number of ways to arrange the 4 math majors within their block:\n   \\[\n   5! \\times 4! = 120 \\times 24 = 2880\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculating the Probability:**\n   The probability is the ratio of the number of favorable arrangements to the total number of arrangements:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Probability} = \\frac{2880}{40320} = \\frac{1}{14}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{14}}\n\\]",
